Romanian law does not name hitchhiking (''autostopul''), so on ordinary roads it is legal; the motorway is where it is not. [https://legislatie.just.ro/Public/DetaliiDocument/74028 Article 74(1) of Government Emergency Ordinance 195/2002] prohibits pedestrians on an ''autostradă'' outright, in the same breath as animals, hand-drawn vehicles, bicycles, mopeds and anything unable to exceed 50 km/h. Article 74(3) forbids driving, stopping or standing on the ''bandă de urgenţă'' — the hard shoulder — other than in justified cases, so no driver can lawfully pull over for you there either. What is left on the motorway network is the signposted service areas, where cars have stopped anyway and you can ask people face to face. On ''drumuri naţionale'', ''drumuri judeţene'' and everything below them, thumbing from the verge breaks no rule, and that is where hitchhiking in Romania actually happens.
